                {"id": "a1ap29", "statement": "Gn is pronounced as two separate sounds, \"g\" then \"n\".", "answer": False, "explanation": "Gn is always a single \"ny\" sound."},
                {"id": "a1ap30", "statement": "Gli is pronounced \"ly\", as in the English word \"million\".", "answer": True, "explanation": "Gli is always the \"ly\" sound."},
                {"id": "a1ap31", "statement": "Every Italian vowel is pronounced clearly, with no reduction like English \"the\" becoming \"thuh\".", "answer": True, "explanation": "Italian vowels are always pronounced fully and clearly."},
                {"id": "a1ap32", "statement": "The Italian alphabet includes the letters j, k, w, x and y as native letters.", "answer": False, "explanation": "These only appear in loanwords like jeans or whisky, not native Italian words."},
